# User documentation

The user documentation lives in `docs/guide/` (English, the source of truth) and `docs/guide.ja/` (its Japanese translation, same folders and file names), and is built into the site in `website/` (design §20.7, decisions 0.116 and 0.117). A pull request that adds or changes an English page changes the Japanese page in the same pull request. `docs/design.ja.md` stays the specification: the guide is written from it, not translated from it, and when the two differ the design is right and the guide is fixed. Before writing, read `.agents/skills/english-writing/SKILL.md`.

## One kind per page

Each page is one of the four Diátaxis kinds (https://diataxis.fr/start-here/) and lives in its folder:

| Folder | Kind | The page |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `tutorials/` | Tutorial | A lesson: the reader follows every step and ends with something working. No choices, no background beyond one line. |
| `how-to/` | How-to guide | Directions for one goal the reader already has. Steps, with the choices the goal needs. |
| `reference/` | Reference | Facts to look up: every option, field, code, and command, in a fixed order. No steps, no reasons. |
| `explanation/` | Explanation | Why and how it works: the mechanism, the flows, what can go wrong, and what the reader must do. No steps. |

A page that needs another kind links to it instead of growing a second kind.

A Grill or a design review that decides what goes on a page checks it against that page's kind and against "code in a page is taken from a sample" before recording it. V1c8's Grill (Q5) put the per-OS linking steps on the C API page, a Reference page, and the design review passed it. Only the first-time reader review of the implementation found the conflict, together with four gaps in the steps: the unpack, the default x86 prompt, the macOS quarantine, and `app.c`. The steps then moved to V1c3c by an owner decision in the middle of the PR (D1, PR #230).

## What the pages describe

- The pages describe what a reader of the trial does: a reader who got ElasticLM from a GitHub prerelease (design §20.7, V1c3e). A tutorial has the reader download the executables from the prerelease's archives and the packages from its `.nupkg` files, as the reader types it, and the step runner (`eng/docs/Test-GuideSteps.ps1`) puts the job's executables and packages in their place at the `steps:prebuilt` markers. A page is not written as "not released yet, so build it from the source": V1c3a and V1c3b wrote the tutorials that way, which left every tutorial to be rewritten just before the release, with no point at which the documentation was checked and done (the owner's finding in PR #218).
- Before the first prerelease there is nothing to download. One sentence in the status of the entry page (`docs/guide/README.md` and its translation) says that ElasticLM is distributed as GitHub prereleases during the trial, and sends a reader who finds none to "Build from source" in `CONTRIBUTING.md`. No other page says that nothing is released yet or that a prerelease may be missing.
- A page may state a behavior that a later row builds (for example, that a prerelease pushes no package or image, which V1e makes true in `release.yml`). Until that row lands, the owner's steps that would make the statement false (`docs/releasing.md`) say not to take them, and name the row. In PR #220 the pages said that an rc pushes nothing, while `release.yml` still pushed every `v*` tag to nuget.org and GHCR; the accuracy review found it, and `docs/releasing.md` gained the line that no `v*` tag is pushed before V1e's change.

## Shape of a page

- The first heading is the page title. Under it, one paragraph says who the page is for and what they should know first.
- An explanation page goes: what it is for → how it works, with a figure → what can go wrong and what the reader must do.
- A reference page is written from the code: every name, value, limit, default, and condition is read where the code defines or checks it, not only in the design. Where the design says otherwise, the same PR fixes the design. In V1c2a (PR #199), pages written from the design had 16 errors that the accuracy review found, and 10 design sections disagreed with the code.
- A statement of how an operating system or another tool behaves (a trust store, an environment variable, an outside command) is tried before it goes into a page, or the PR says that it was not tried. Statements about ElasticLM's own behavior are read in its code. In PR #257 the TLS page first said that a process given `SSL_CERT_FILE` trusts no other CA, and that the server sends the intermediate CA certificates of its PEM file; a run in a .NET 10 container showed the system's CAs still trusted, and the code (`X509Certificate2.CreateFromPemFile`) reads only the first certificate, which became row V1c12.
- When the step runner prepares something for a line that a reader must prepare too (a folder on `PATH`, an environment variable, a tool on the runner), the page gives the reader that step. CI runs with what the runner prepared, so it cannot notice the step missing from the page. In PR #265 the NativeAOT page left out the Visual Studio Installer folder on `PATH`, which the runner adds to its `dotnet` lines on Windows, and read the resulting `vswhere.exe` error as a missing workload; the accuracy review (C1) and the first-time reader (F1) found it, and a publish without that folder on `PATH` failed with `MSB3073` on a developer's machine.
- Name the threat when the page says something is safe: who the attacker is, what they hold, and what stops them. Write what the product does not protect against as plainly as what it does.
- Advice in a page about keys or secrets (which passphrase to use, where a key is used, what is kept where) agrees with the pages that set the rules for them: the root key page, the security model page, and `SECURITY.md`. The writer checks each piece of advice against those pages before the reviews. In PR #253 the rotation page told the reader to give the new signing key the passphrase of the old one (in the tutorial, also that of the root key) and to certify it with the root key on the service's machine; the root key page of the same PR forbids both. Neither the design review nor the accuracy review found it; the first-time reader review did (F2, F3).
- A figure is a fenced `mermaid` block: a sequence diagram for a protocol, a state diagram for a lifecycle, a flowchart for an order of checks. No ASCII art and no images of text.
- The site job of `release.yml` opens every page of the built site, `/ja/` included, in the light and the dark color scheme, and fails when a page has fewer or more diagrams than the `mermaid` blocks of its source page, or one without its `svg` (`website/check-mermaid.mjs`, design §20.7, V1c5). In V1c1 a second flowchart rendered empty only in the dark scheme (Issue #192).
- A flowchart label that holds parentheses, brackets, or braces is written in double quotes: `S{"Does the license limit concurrent users (seats)?"}`. Without the quotes Mermaid reads them as the syntax of a node's shape, the diagram does not parse, and its container stays empty. In V1c5 the site job's check failed on this label of the Overview page, written without quotes (run 42646637283, PR #304).
- Code in a page is taken from a sample in `samples/`, which the gate builds (from V1c3 on). A C sample is the exception: the gate needs no C compiler, so the CI `aot` job builds it as its page says, on Windows, Linux, and macOS, and `tests/native/run.ps1` compiles it with warnings as errors (design §20.7, V1c3c, the owner's decision on review R7). Commands are given as they are typed, with fake keys and IDs.
- A tutorial's sample answers a reader's slip (arguments in the wrong order, a missing file, a file that is not a key) with one line and an exit code, not an unhandled exception. The first V1c3a sample threw `ArgumentException` when the two arguments were swapped, and the page explained the stack trace; the first-time reader review found it (PR #214).

## Markdown that works on GitHub and on the site

- Plain CommonMark with GitHub tables and fenced code. No MDX, no JSX, no Docusaurus admonitions (`:::note`), no front matter beyond `sidebar_position` and `title` when the folder order needs them.
- Links are relative paths to `.md` files (`../explanation/trust-chain.md#rotation`). A link to a page that does not exist yet is not written: the row that writes that page adds the link. The site build fails on a broken link or anchor.
- A link to a file outside `docs/guide/` (`SECURITY.md`, `docs/container.md`) is a full GitHub URL on the `main` branch.

## Words

- Terms follow the table in `.agents/skills/english-writing/SKILL.md`. The first use of a term on a page says what it is in a few words or links to the page that explains it.
- Wire names, settings, error codes, and commands are in code spans exactly as the code has them. Check each one in the code before it goes in.
- A figure (a size, a time, a default) is checked in the design or the code before it goes in. The PR names the source; the page does not.
- Write for users, not for this repository's developers. A page carries no design section numbers, decision numbers (`0.xx`), threat or attacker IDs (`T12`, `A3`), roadmap rows, row names (`V1b`), source file paths, the names of tests, "in the code", or stories of earlier attempts. Say the fact itself. When the design and the code differ, the page follows the code and the PR fixes the design.
- One name for one thing across all pages: "administration API" (not "management API"); policy settings and values as the administration API spells them (`maxActivations`, `cloneStrategy`: `deny`); settings as the configuration file spells them (`rateLimit.clientPerIp`).
- A caveat that several pages share (revocation reaches a device only when its token expires, a patched app binary is out of scope, offline copies are not detected) is explained once, on the page it belongs to, and the other pages say it in a sentence and link there.

## Japanese pages

- A faithful translation of the English page: the same facts, sections, figures, tables, and links. Nothing added, dropped, strengthened, or weakened. An error found in the English is fixed in the English first.
- Terms follow the glossary below, not the design's Japanese: the design's terms were coined to record decisions and read unnaturally to users (owner's decision, Issue #190). Plain form (常体), written and checked with `.agents/skills/natural-japanese/`, without stiff Sino-Japanese words (本ドキュメント, 〜を実施する, 当該) and without asking the reader (〜してほしい). Code, commands, settings, wire names, and error codes stay in English.
- Mermaid labels are translated; node, state, and participant IDs stay ASCII.
- A link to a heading uses the Japanese page's own heading; the site build checks it.
- A review that changes a term (one word for another) is applied by searching the whole page, and the other pages the change touches, for the old word, not by rewriting the lines the review named. In V1c14 the Japanese review (J1) changed 写し to コピー, the edit rewrote the named lines only, and the exit-code row of `backup` kept 写し until the owner's review (PR #273, S2).

