Phrases that read as translations, and what to write instead: "consume a seat" シートを使う (not 消費する), "release a seat" シートを返す, "bound to a machine" マシンに紐付ける (not 縛られる), "fail silently" what happens instead (not 静かに失敗する), "enforce" 制限する・確かめる (not 強制する).

## Checks before a PR

- `corepack pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` and `corepack pnpm run build` in `website/` pass, and so does `node check-mermaid.mjs` there when a page's diagrams changed (`CONTRIBUTING.md` says how to run it). On Windows a page of that check can fail to load with `net::ERR_NO_BUFFER_SPACE`; the check prints a `retry` line and opens the page once more. In V1c5 this happened in one of seven runs made one after another with Microsoft Edge 157, and the retry passed; the cause was not traced (PR #304).
- Two independent reviewers read the pages (design §20.7, V1c1): one is given only `docs/guide/` and `README.md` and reports what a first-time reader cannot follow; the other is given the design and the code and reports every statement that differs from them.
- A review that runs a page's steps before the first prerelease gets executables published as the CI's `aot` job publishes them and packages packed with a prerelease suffix (`-p:VersionSuffix=ci.<number>`), puts them into `tools` and `packages` as the page says, and only reads the download paragraphs.
- The Japanese pages are written, or brought up to date, after both reviews of the English pages are fixed. In V1c2a (PR #199) they were translated first, and every page was translated a second time after the reviews.
- After translating, compare the code blocks and code spans of each Japanese page with the English byte for byte. In V1c2b a writing tool turned the `'` escapes of a JSON example into the characters themselves; the comparison found it.
- A reviewer other than the translator checks the Japanese pages for natural Japanese and for any difference from the English.
- A page whose CI run stops before a step that needs a person (approving a device code, signing in in a browser) has the rest of its steps run once by the correctness review or the first-time reader, with `wslc` and HTTP requests in place of the person. In V1c4d2b CI stopped at `/auth/login`, and only the correctness review, which ran the device flow on the page's realm, found that the realm's user lacked the default roles, so `elm admin login` failed (PR #285, C1).
- To run an English page's steps before its Japanese page exists, put a copy of the English page at the Japanese path for the run: the step runner refuses a page without a translation (`has no translation at …`). The copy is never committed; the translation replaces it (V1c4d1, PR #277).
- A value of a step marker (`pattern`, `capture`) holds no quote, so a check of JSON output captures a word that names the value, such as the license's name or the product's code, not the JSON around it. In V1c4d1 `pattern="\"name\":\"(customer-a)\""` failed as a marker error, and `pattern="(customer-a)"` replaced it (PR #277).
- A change that adds a command which shares a rule with a command already documented (it reads the same settings, needs the same files, or takes the same exit codes) searches the Reference pages for the statements that name the earlier command, the defaults columns of the settings tables included, and changes them in both languages. In V1c14 `backup` reads the settings as `setup` does; the design's table said so, while five rows of the settings table of `service-configuration.md` still said "`setup` does not need it" until the owner's review (PR #273, S1).
- A statement about what a program does at run time that no test checks (an environment variable changes the log level, a file appears at the start) is tried once by running the program, and the PR says how. In V1c2a the review of PR #199 found `Logging__LogLevel__Default` stated without a run; starting `elm-server` and `elm-service` with `Warning` then showed no `info` lines, against 10 without it.
- Before choosing the case of an output example, read the checks that the command itself makes before the step that prints the line: a case that an earlier check stops never reaches it. In V1c17 the example of `elm server activate` keeping the server's license used a server clock behind the service's, which `elm`'s own verification of the answer refuses first (PR #311).
- An example taken from a real run comes from programs copied to a scratch folder when other work builds the same projects at the time: Windows cannot build over a running executable. In V1c2b two page writers ran copied programs while the code changed beside them.
- The PR body of a change to pages gives the steps to see them locally (`CONTRIBUTING.md`) and the URLs of the changed pages in both languages. In V1c2a and V1c2b the owner had to ask how to look at the pages before the review.
